"""
Elapsed time since the program started, accumulated on the clock.

A purely discrete program has no continuous `time` to read, so anything that needs
a schedule counts sample times instead. The first tick reports `Ts`, not 0.

Clock-agnostic.
"""
component ElapsedTime@[input clk extends Discrete]
  "Time since the first tick [s]"
  y = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^y}
  "Sample time (defaults to SampleTime())"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = SampleTime()
relations
  initial y@(clk-1) = 0
  y@clk = y@(clk-1) + Ts
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/ElapsedTime.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "y": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    }
  }
}
end

"""
Staircase velocity reference that sweeps a range of speeds and then repeats it in
the other direction.

The friction experiment needs the axis held at a *constant* velocity long enough
for the acceleration to be negligible, so that `τ = J α + f(ω)` collapses to
`τ = f(ω)` and the inertia never has to be modelled. It also needs both signs,
because Coulomb friction is what the experiment is mostly after and a one-sided
sweep cannot separate it from an offset.

So the reference holds `n_levels` speeds spanning `w_min` to `w_max` for `t_step`
seconds each, then flips sign and sweeps the same speeds again. One full cycle
therefore takes `2 * n_levels * t_step` seconds, after which it repeats.

The speeds are spaced as `(level / (n_levels - 1)) ^ spacing`, i.e. **quadratically**
by default rather than evenly. That is deliberate: everything the friction fit is
really after — the Coulomb breakaway and the steep part of the curve — lives at low
speed, so that is where the resolution is wanted, while evenly spaced levels would
spend half the experiment at high speeds that only pin down the cubic term. With
`w_min = 1`, `w_max = 40`, `n_levels = 10` the steps come out roughly 0.5 rad/s apart
near the bottom and 8 rad/s apart near the top. `spacing = 1` gives even spacing back.

Either way `level = 0` yields exactly `w_min` and `level = n_levels - 1` exactly
`w_max`, so changing `spacing` redistributes the speeds without moving the endpoints.

`level` and `dir` are held in delayed variables and advanced by an edge detector on
the elapsed time rather than computed from it with `floor`/`mod`: it makes the block
a plain discrete state machine of arithmetic and `ifelse`, which is what keeps it
compiling for the C backend as readily as for Julia.

Clock-agnostic.
"""
component VelocityStaircase@[input clk extends Discrete]
  "Elapsed time [s], e.g. from `ElapsedTime`"
  t_in = Dyad.RealInput@[clk]() {^t_in}
  "Velocity reference [rad/s]"
  w_ref = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^w_ref}
  "Slowest speed in the sweep"
  parameter w_min::Dyad.AngularVelocity = 2.0
  "Fastest speed in the sweep"
  parameter w_max::Dyad.AngularVelocity = 30.0
  "Number of speeds per direction"
  parameter n_levels::Real = 6
  "Time held at each speed [s]"
  parameter t_step::Dyad.Time = 2.0
  "Speed-distribution exponent: 2 clusters the levels at low speed, 1 spaces them evenly"
  parameter spacing::Real = 2
  "Completed steps"
  variable idx::Real
  "Index of the current speed, 0 to n_levels-1"
  variable level::Real
  "Current direction, +1 or -1"
  variable dir::Real
  "1 on the tick a new step begins, else 0"
  variable advance::Real
relations
  initial idx@(clk-1) = 0
  initial level@(clk-1) = 0
  initial dir@(clk-1) = 1
  # Edge detector: fires on the first tick at or past the next step boundary.
  advance@clk = ifelse(t_in@clk >= (idx@(clk-1) + 1) * t_step, 1, 0)
  idx@clk = idx@(clk-1) + advance@clk
  # Walk up the levels; on wrapping past the top level, restart at the bottom in
  # the opposite direction.
  level@clk = ifelse(advance@clk > 0.5, ifelse(level@(clk-1) + 1 >= n_levels, 0, level@(clk-1) + 1), level@(clk-1))
  dir@clk = ifelse(advance@clk > 0.5, ifelse(level@(clk-1) + 1 >= n_levels, -dir@(clk-1), dir@(clk-1)), dir@(clk-1))
  w_ref@clk = dir@clk * (w_min + (w_max - w_min) * (level@clk / max(n_levels - 1, 1)) ^ spacing)
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/VelocityStaircase.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "t_in": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": -100, "x2": 0, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    },
    "w_ref": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    }
  }
}
end

"""
Writes one row of the connected signals to a file on every tick, from inside the
synchronous program.

The row is appended by `log_row`, a call out to C (`csrc/qube_log.c`, via
`src/data_log.jl`), so this logs from the Julia backend, the `:c` backend and
exported standalone C alike — the same arrangement `HardwareMeasurement` and
`HardwareCommand` use for the device I/O. No data leaves through the component's
interface: the file is the output.

`n` sets how many inputs there are, so connect `u[1]` through `u[n]`. `filename` and
`header` are **structural** parameters, i.e. build-time Julia values that never enter
the equations — a filename is a string and every signal crossing a synchronous node's
interface is a `double`, so the file is opened by the driver (`open_log!`) with the
values the model was built with rather than by the program itself.

`row` is the number of the row just written, or 0 when no log is open — in which
case the component is inert and the model runs unchanged.

`log_row` has a fixed arity of eight because a registered operator must: there is no
variable-argument form, and passing an array instead would put an array-valued signal
inside the clocked partition. So `v` pads the `n` inputs out to eight, and the columns
past `n` are discarded because the C side writes only the `ncols` it was opened with.

The padding is `0.0` and not `0` on purpose, and this one bites: mixing element types
across an array variable's equations — some `Float64`, some the integer literal Dyad
emits for `= 0` — makes SynchToolkit materialise `v` with `vcat` into a
`SynchJulia.SynchArray`, which SynchCompiler's C lowering then rejects as non-isbits.
All-`Float64` elements compile on both backends. Nothing in the Dyad source hints that
`0` and `0.0` differ, and the error surfaces two packages away naming only
`Tuple{typeof(vcat), Float64, …, Int64}`, so keep the zeros floating.

`u`'s placement carries `show: 8`, which is what fans an array connector out into one
sub-connector per element across its bounding box (a tall band down the left edge
here) instead of collapsing it to a single point. The kernel has honoured that since
June 2026; `dyad-ui` 0.12.1, which the headless diagram exporter bundles, does not, so
PNGs from that route still draw all `n` connections converging.

Clock-agnostic.
"""
component DataLogger@[input clk extends Discrete]
  "Signals to log, one column each"
  u = [Dyad.RealInput@[clk]() for i in 1:n] {^u}
  "Number of the row just written, 0 if no log is open"
  row = Dyad.RealOutput@[clk]() {^row}
  "Number of signals logged, 1 to 8"
  structural parameter n::Integer = 1
  "File the rows are written to, opened by the driver with this name"
  structural parameter filename::String = "log.csv"
  "Tab-separated column names written as the first line; empty writes no header"
  structural parameter header::String = ""
  "The inputs, padded out to the operator's fixed arity"
  variable v::Real[8]
relations
  for i in 1:n
    v[i] = u[i]
  end
  for i in n + 1:8
    v[i] = 0.0
  end
  row@clk = log_row(v[1], v[2], v[3], v[4], v[5], v[6], v[7], v[8])
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/DataLogger.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "u": {
      "Dyad": {
        "placement": {"diagram": {"show": 8, "x1": -100, "x2": 0, "y1": 60, "y2": 940, "show": 8}}
      }
    },
    "row": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    }
  }
}
end

"""
Speed-dependent resisting torque on a rotational axis: Coulomb friction, back-EMF and
the higher-order terms, as one law.

The name is the point. A constant-velocity experiment measures the *command* a speed
costs, and that single number cannot separate mechanical friction from the motor's
back-EMF -- both are volts per rad/s. Splitting them means fitting one and subtracting a
`km` obtained elsewhere, and when the two disagree the remainder comes out
non-dissipative. So this block is the whole speed-dependent torque of the axis and
`DCMotor` deliberately has no back-EMF term.

```math
τ_f(ω) = k_c σ(ω) + k_v ω + k_2 σ(ω) ω^2 + k_3 ω^3
```

with `σ(ω) = tanh(ω / w_tanh)`, or the hard `sign(ω)` when `smooth = false`. Every term
is odd in `ω`, so `τ_f(-ω) = -τ_f(ω)`: the torque always opposes the motion. The two
terms carrying `σ` are the friction-like ones; `k_v ω` is where back-EMF lands, being
exactly proportional to speed. Smoothing is the default because a hard `sign` at `ω = 0` is both
numerically awkward and a poor description of what the device does at a standstill;
`w_tanh` is the width of the transition, in rad/s.

`tau_f` is the magnitude of the *opposing* torque, i.e. positive for positive `w`, so
it enters an equation of motion with a minus sign: `J ω̇ = τ_motor - tau_f`.
The name avoids colliding with the flange torque `tau` that
[`RotationalFriction`](@ref) inherits from the rotational interface.

The coefficients come from a [`FrictionParams`](@ref) set, so a whole fit can be
selected in one line — `FrictionAndBackEMF(params = friction_identified)` — the same
arrangement `QubePendulum` uses for `IdParams`. Fit them from a constant-velocity
experiment with `FurutaFrictionExperiment` and
examples/friction_identification.jl.

`QubePendulum` has no separate viscous coefficient and no back-EMF beside this. Do not
read the coefficients individually: `k_v` is friction *and* back-EMF together, and with
`k_2`/`k_3` present none of them is separately meaningful — judge a set by `τ_f(ω)` over
the range it was measured on. What the model buys over a plain damper is `k_c`, which is
why the arm needs more voltage to start moving than to keep moving.

A compensator built from this must skip `k_v`: cancelling it would cancel the motor's
back-EMF, which is real damping worth keeping. `SwingupCatch` instantiates its
feedforward with `kv = 0` for that reason.
"""
component FrictionAndBackEMF
  "Angular velocity of the axis [rad/s]"
  w = Dyad.RealInput() {^w}
  "Friction torque opposing the motion [N·m]"
  tau_f = Dyad.RealOutput() {^tau_f}
  "Coefficient set (see dyad/definitions.jl: `friction_nominal`, `friction_identified`)"
  structural parameter params::Native = friction_nominal
  "Smooth the sign with tanh instead of using a hard sign"
  structural parameter smooth::Boolean = true
  "Coulomb (breakaway) torque"
  parameter kc::Dyad.Torque = params.kc
  "First-order coefficient: friction and back-EMF together"
  parameter kv::Dyad.RotationalDampingConstant = params.kv
  "Quadratic coefficient"
  parameter k2::Real = params.k2
  "Cubic coefficient"
  parameter k3::Real = params.k3
  "Width of the smoothed sign transition [rad/s] (only used if `smooth = true`)"
  parameter w_tanh::Dyad.AngularVelocity = params.w_tanh
  "Sign of the velocity, smoothed or not"
  variable sw::Real
relations
  # A structural switch, not `ifelse`: `ifelse` evaluates both branches, so the
  # tanh would divide by zero whenever a hard sign is what was asked for.
  if smooth
    sw = tanh(w / w_tanh)
  else
    sw = sign(w)
  end
  tau_f = kc * sw + kv * w + k2 * sw * w ^ 2 + k3 * w*abs(w ^ 2)
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/Friction.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "w": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": -100, "x2": 0, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    },
    "tau_f": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 1000, "x2": 1100, "y1": 450, "y2": 550}}}
    }
  }
}
end

"""
[`FrictionAndBackEMF`](@ref) as a rotational two-port, so it drops in exactly where a
`Damper` would: connect it between a joint's `axis` and `support` and nothing else changes.
`Damper` contributes `tau = d * w_rel`; this contributes the whole friction law.

It *contains* a `FrictionAndBackEMF` rather than extending it: extending would inherit
that block's `w` and `tau_f` signal connectors, and a rotational two-port should present
flanges and nothing else. So the law and the `FrictionParams` plumbing still have exactly
one definition in the package, and the two bridging equations tie it to the flange
variables supplied by the same rotational interface `Damper` uses.

The coefficients are re-declared here only to forward them with `final`, which keeps
`RotationalFrictionAndBackEMF(params = friction_identified)` working and still lets an individual
coefficient be overridden.
"""
component RotationalFrictionAndBackEMF
  extends RotationalComponents.Interfaces.PartialCompliantWithRelativeStates
  friction = QuanserComponents.FrictionAndBackEMF(params = params, smooth = smooth, final kc = kc, final kv = kv, final k2 = k2, final k3 = k3, final w_tanh = w_tanh) {^friction}
  "Coefficient set (see dyad/definitions.jl: `friction_nominal`, `friction_identified`)"
  structural parameter params::Native = friction_nominal
  "Smooth the sign with tanh instead of using a hard sign"
  structural parameter smooth::Boolean = true
  "Coulomb (breakaway) torque"
  parameter kc::Dyad.Torque = params.kc
  "First-order coefficient"
  parameter kv::Dyad.RotationalDampingConstant = params.kv
  "Quadratic coefficient"
  parameter k2::Real = params.k2
  "Cubic coefficient"
  parameter k3::Real = params.k3
  "Width of the smoothed sign transition [rad/s] (only used if `smooth = true`)"
  parameter w_tanh::Dyad.AngularVelocity = params.w_tanh
relations
  friction.w = w_rel
  tau = friction.tau_f
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/Friction.svg"}},
  "_links": {"friction": {}}
}
end

"""
Constant-velocity friction experiment on the physical QUBE, as a single synchronous
program.

The arm is driven around a staircase of constant speeds by a PI velocity
controller, and every tick a row of `[time, w_ref, shoulder_angle,
shoulder_velocity, control_input, elbow_angle]` is written to `log_file`. Fit
`FrictionParams` to that log with examples/friction_identification.jl; run the
experiment with the `FurutaFrictionExperiment` analysis.

Everything is inside the program: `HardwareMeasurement` reads the encoders,
`HardwareCommand` writes the motor, `DataLogger` writes the file. So the driver
supplies nothing but a clock tick — see `generate_friction_controller` and
`FrictionController` in src/friction.jl.

`K` and `Ti` are parameters *here*, bound down into `velocity_pi` with `final`, so that
the `FurutaFrictionExperiment` analysis can forward its own `K`/`Ti` into the model the
ordinary way (`model = FurutaFriction(final K = K, final Ti = Ti, ...)`). They are also
the compiled program's runtime-settable `TuningGains`, which requires them to be
*unbound* — hence root parameters bound downwards rather than `velocity_pi`'s own.
Because the binding makes `velocity_pi.K` an expression in `K`, SynchToolkit substitutes
it away entirely: the node reads one `K`, with no second copy in `AutoPars` that could
fall out of step. So `FrictionController(; K, Ti)` retunes without recompiling.

Two things about the experiment that matter more than the model does:

  * **Leave deadband compensation as the controller has it.** The driver adds a fixed
    offset in the direction of the command, which lands directly on top of the Coulomb
    term this experiment measures, so `kc` comes out as the *residual* after that
    compensation rather than as mechanical Coulomb friction — small, and occasionally
    small enough to go negative if the driver over-compensates.

    That is the right number anyway, and switching the compensation off to get a
    "purer" one is a trap this experiment fell into once: with compensation off, `kc`
    grows to cover the amplifier deadband (~0.57 V of command), and the fitted set then
    describes a plant no controller run uses. Nothing in the loop compensates a
    deadband — the feedforward here is odd in speed, a deadband is odd in command, and
    the two coincide only in a constant-velocity sweep, never at the top where the speed
    crosses zero while the command must act. Measured on the rig: holding upright takes
    ~0.1 V and 0.2 deg of error with compensation on, ~0.59 V and 1.9 deg with it off.
    So run the sweep on the same command-to-torque path the controller uses, which is
    what `card_options = ""` (the default) does.
  * **The pendulum is a disturbance here.** At a genuinely constant arm speed it
    contributes no torque about the shoulder axis, but it takes a long time to get
    there — `bp` is tiny — so it rings through most of each step. The inertia-disc
    configuration gives a much cleaner fit if the disc is at hand. Otherwise the
    logged `elbow_angle` is there so the identification script can keep only the
    samples where the pendulum is also quiet.
"""
component FurutaFriction
  measurement = HardwareMeasurement() {^measurement}
  elapsed = ElapsedTime() {^elapsed}
  reference = VelocityStaircase(final w_min = w_min, final w_max = w_max, final n_levels = n_levels, final t_step = t_step, final spacing = spacing) {^reference}
  velocityestimator = VelocityEstimator(final filter_param = filter_param) {^velocityestimator}
  velocity_pi = DiscreteComponents.DiscretePIDStandard(with_D = false, final K = K, final Ti = Ti, Ni = 1.0, final y_max = umax, wp = 1.2) {^velocity_pi}
  command = HardwareCommand(final umax = umax) {^command}
  logger = DataLogger(n = FRICTION_LOG_NCOLS, filename = log_file, header = FRICTION_LOG_HEADER) {^logger}
  periodicclock = DiscreteComponents.PeriodicClock(dt = Ts) {^periodicclock}
  "Sample time [s]"
  structural parameter Ts::Real = 0.005
  "File the log is written to; the driver opens it with this name"
  structural parameter log_file::String = FRICTION_LOG_FILE
  "Motor saturation [V]"
  parameter umax::Dyad.Voltage = 10.0
  "Velocity-loop proportional gain [V·s/rad]"
  parameter K::Real = 0.05
  "Velocity-loop integral time [s]; must be well above Ts, the integrator is forward-Euler"
  parameter Ti::Dyad.Time = 0.5
  "Velocity-estimator filter parameter; small means more filtering"
  parameter filter_param::Real(min = 0, max = 1) = 0.1
  "Slowest speed in the sweep [rad/s]"
  parameter w_min::Dyad.AngularVelocity = 2.0
  "Fastest speed in the sweep [rad/s]"
  parameter w_max::Dyad.AngularVelocity = 30.0
  "Number of speeds per direction"
  parameter n_levels::Real = 6
  "Time held at each speed [s]"
  parameter t_step::Dyad.Time = 2.0
  "Speed-distribution exponent: 2 clusters the levels at low speed, 1 spaces them evenly"
  parameter spacing::Real = 2
relations
  connect(velocity_pi.y, command.u) {^id9}
  # Logging, in the FRICTION_LOG_COLUMNS order
  connect(elapsed.y, logger.u[1], reference.t_in) {^id10}
  connect(reference.w_ref, logger.u[2], velocity_pi.u_s) {^id11}
  connect(measurement.shoulder_angle, logger.u[3], velocityestimator.pos) {^id12}
  connect(velocityestimator.vel, logger.u[4], velocity_pi.u_m) {^id13}
  connect(command.u_applied, logger.u[5]) {^id14}
  connect(measurement.elbow_angle, logger.u[6], periodicclock.y) {^id15}
metadata {
  "Dyad": {"icons": {"default": "dyad://QuanserComponents/FurutaFriction.svg"}},
  "_links": {
    "measurement": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 50, "x2": 150, "y1": 660, "y2": 760}}}
    },
    "elapsed": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 30, "x2": 130, "y1": 430, "y2": 530}}}
    },
    "reference": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 240, "x2": 340, "y1": 430, "y2": 530}}}
    },
    "velocityestimator": {
      "Dyad": {"placement": {"diagram": {"x1": 240, "x2": 340, "y1": 560, "y2": 660}}}
    },
